examples.question_text:
A semicircle is joined to the right side of a rectangle. Explain why $P=l+2r$ does not apply, then calculate the perimeter to 1 decimal place.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(0,4)--(8,4) arc[start angle=90,end angle=-90,radius=2] --cycle;
\draw[black,dashed] (8,0)--(8,4);
\path (4,0) node[below] {$8\text{ cm}$} (0,2) node[left] {$4\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The straight boundary consists of three rectangle sides, so this is not a sector. The dashed diameter is inside the shape.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=5mm,y=5mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(0,4)--(8,4) arc[start angle=90,end angle=-90,radius=2] --cycle;
\draw[black,dashed] (8,0)--(8,4);
\path (4,0) node[below] {$8\text{ cm}$} (4,4) node[above] {$8\text{ cm}$} (0,2) node[left] {$4\text{ cm}$} (10,2) node[right] {$l$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}P&=8+8+4+l\\&=20+\frac12\times\pi(4)\\&\approx26.3\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

examples.question_text:
A quadrant of radius $5$ m is joined to a rectangle, as shown. Calculate the perimeter to 2 decimal places.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(0,5)--(7,5) arc[start angle=90,end angle=0,radius=5] --cycle;
\draw[black,dashed] (7,0)--(7,5);
\path (3.5,5) node[above] {$7\text{ m}$} (0,2.5) node[left] {$5\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The quadrant adds a $5$ m straight section to the bottom. Its other radius is inside the shape.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(0,5)--(7,5) arc[start angle=90,end angle=0,radius=5] --cycle;
\draw[black,dashed] (7,0)--(7,5);
\path (3.5,5) node[above] {$7\text{ m}$} (0,2.5) node[left] {$5\text{ m}$} (3.5,0) node[below] {$7\text{ m}$} (9.5,0) node[below] {$5\text{ m}$} (11,4) node[above right] {$l$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}P&=7+5+7+5+l\\&=24+\frac14\times2\pi(5)\\&\approx31.85\text{ m}\end{aligned}$

examples.question_text:
A quarter circle of radius $6$ cm is cut from a square of side $6$ cm. Find the exact perimeter of the remaining piece.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (6,0)--(6,6)--(0,6) arc[start angle=90,end angle=0,radius=6];
\draw[black,dashed] (0,6)--(0,0)--(6,0);
\path (3,6) node[above] {$6\text{ cm}$} (6,3) node[right] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The cut removes the left and bottom sides completely. The remaining boundary has two straight sides and a quarter-circle arc.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=6mm,y=6mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (6,0)--(6,6)--(0,6) arc[start angle=90,end angle=0,radius=6];
\draw[black,dashed] (0,6)--(0,0)--(6,0);
\path (3,6) node[above] {$6\text{ cm}$} (6,3) node[right] {$6\text{ cm}$} (3.7,3.7) node[below left] {$l$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}P&=6+6+l\\&=12+\frac14\times2\pi(6)\\&=(12+3\pi)\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

examples.question_text:
A track has two straight sections of $18$ m and two semicircular ends, each of diameter $10$ m. Calculate its perimeter to 1 decimal place.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=2mm,y=2mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (0,0)--(18,0) arc[start angle=-90,end angle=90,radius=5] --(0,10) arc[start angle=90,end angle=270,radius=5];
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(0,10);
\path (9,0) node[below] {$18\text{ m}$} (9,10) node[above] {$18\text{ m}$};
\draw[black,<->] (-8,0)--(-8,10);
\path (-8,5) node[left] {$10\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A small semicircle is cut from a large semicircle. Both curved edges are semicircles. Find the exact perimeter of the remaining shape.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (12,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,radius=6] --(6,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,radius=3];
\draw[black,dashed] (6,0)--(12,0);
\path (3,0) node[below] {$6\text{ cm}$} (9,0) node[below] {$6\text{ cm}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
The large diameter is $6+6=12$ cm. The small diameter is $6$ cm. Both arcs count, but only the left straight section counts.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (12,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,radius=6] --(6,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,radius=3];
\draw[black,dashed] (6,0)--(12,0);
\path (3,0) node[below] {$6\text{ cm}$} (9,0) node[below] {$6\text{ cm}$} (6,6) node[above] {$l_1$} (9,3) node[below] {$l_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
$\begin{aligned}P&=6+l_1+l_2\\&=6+\frac12\times\pi(12)+\frac12\times\pi(6)\\&=(6+9\pi)\text{ cm}\end{aligned}$

examples.question_text:
The curved top is an arc centred at $O$, the midpoint of the base. Its radius is $15$ mm and its central angle is approximately $73.7^\circ$. Use $73.7^\circ$ to calculate the perimeter to 2 decimal places.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=3mm,y=3mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (-9,0)--(9,0)--(9,12) arc[start angle=53.130102,end angle=126.869898,radius=15] --cycle;
\draw[black,dashed] (-9,12)--(0,0)--(9,12);
\draw[black] (53.130102:4) arc[start angle=53.130102,end angle=126.869898,radius=4];
\path (0,0) node[below] {$18\text{ mm}$} (-9,6) node[left] {$12\text{ mm}$} (9,6) node[right] {$12\text{ mm}$} (0,5.7) node {$\approx73.7^\circ$} (6,8) node[fill=white,inner sep=1pt] {$15\text{ mm}$} (0,0) node[above left] {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

A semicircular path has outer radius $6.5$ m and inner radius $2.5$ m. Calculate its perimeter to 2 decimal places.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (6.5,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,radius=6.5] --(-2.5,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,radius=2.5] --cycle;
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(120:6.5);
\draw[black,dashed] (0,0)--(35:2.5);
\path (120:4) node[left] {$6.5\text{ m}$} (35:1.5) node[below right] {$2.5\text{ m}$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

examples.solution_text:
Each straight end is the difference between the radii.
$\begin{aligned}s&=6.5-2.5\\&=4\text{ m}\end{aligned}$
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[x=4mm,y=4mm,every node/.style={font=\fontsize{10}{12}\selectfont,text=black}]
\draw[black] (6.5,0) arc[start angle=0,end angle=180,radius=6.5] --(-2.5,0) arc[start angle=180,end angle=0,radius=2.5] --cycle;
\path (-4.5,0) node[below] {$4\text{ m}$} (4.5,0) node[below] {$4\text{ m}$} (0,6.5) node[above] {$l_1$} (0,2.5) node[below] {$l_2$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Both semicircular arcs are on the boundary.
$\begin{aligned}P&=4+4+l_1+l_2\\&=8+\frac12\times2\pi(6.5)+\frac12\times2\pi(2.5)\\&=8+9\pi\\&\approx36.27\text{ m}\end{aligned}$
